{"name":"Royal Wedding","hex":"#fbe3e3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"royal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to a monarch or his (or her) family.","Having the air or demeanour of a monarch; illustrious; magnanimous; of more than common size or excellence."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A royal person; a member of a royal family.","A standard size of printing paper, measuring 25 by 20 inches."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/royal"},{"term":"wedding","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A marriage ceremony; a ritual officially celebrating the beginning of a marriage.","The joining of two or more parts."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of wed"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wedding"}],"entries":[{"title":"Royal Wedding","blocks":[{"text":"Royal Wedding is a 1951 American musical comedy film directed by Stanley Donen and starring Fred Astaire and Jane Powell, with music by Burton Lane and lyrics by Alan Jay Lerner. Set in 1947 London at the time of the wedding of Princess Elizabeth and Philip Mountbatten, the film follows an American brother-sister song-and-dance duo, each of whom falls in love—the brother with a female dancer and the sister with an impoverished but well-connected nobleman. The film marked Donen's second directorial feature. It was released as Wedding Bells in the United Kingdom."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/59/Royal_Wedding_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Wedding","wikidata":"Q538600"},{"title":"Royal Wedding (soundtrack)","blocks":[{"text":"The original soundtrack to the 1951 film Royal Wedding was released by MGM Records in the same year in three formats: as a set of four 10-inch 78-rpm phonograph records, a set of four 45-rpm EPs, and as a 10-inch 33-rpm LP record."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/29/Royal_Wedding_%28soundtrack%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Wedding_(soundtrack)","wikidata":"Q131580863"},{"title":"List of royal weddings","blocks":[{"text":"A royal wedding is a marriage ceremony involving members of a royal family. Weddings involving senior members of the royal family are often seen as important occasions of state and attract significant national and international attention. The following is a list of notable royal weddings:"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/The_marriage_procession_of_Dara_Shikoh_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/330px-The_marriage_procession_of_Dara_Shikoh_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_royal_weddings","wikidata":"Q4992501"},{"title":"Wedding","blocks":[{"text":"A wedding is a ceremony in which two people are united in marriage. Wedding traditions and customs vary greatly between cultures, ethnicities, races, religions, denominations, countries, social classes, and sexual orientations. Most wedding ceremonies involve an exchange of marriage vows by a couple; a presentation of a gift ; and a public proclamation of marriage by an authority figure or celebrant. Special wedding garments are often worn, and the ceremony is sometimes followed by a wedding reception. Music, poetry, prayers, or readings from religious texts or literature are also commonly incorporated into the ceremony, as well as superstitious customs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Hindu_wedding_rituals_b_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Hindu_wedding_rituals_b_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wedding","wikidata":"Q49836"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/com